            <ArticleTitle>Active warming as emergency interventional care for the treatment of pelvic pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1031-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess whether local active warming can lessen acute pelvic pain of gynaecological origin compared with traditional methods in a prehospital setting.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective, randomised, single-blinded study. Setting. Prehospital emergency system. Population. Women calling emergency ambulance for pelvic pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Women were randomised in two groups: resistive heating (group 1) or passive warming (group 2), each treatment was initiated at the emergency site.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Pain on visual analogue scale (VAS), anxiety and nausea, given as mean (SD).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Prior to the interventions, all women were vasoconstricted and had comparable pain scores. Then, group 1 showed a significant (P &lt; 0.01) reduction in pain (VAS: 72.2 [10.5] mm to 32.4 [18.0] mm), anxiety (VAS: 59.0 [10.9] mm to 37.5 [24.1] mm), nausea (VAS: 42.7 [6.2] mm to 21.6 [5.0] mm) and heart rate (101 [12] beats per minute [bpm] to 59 [8] bpm), as well as in the number of vasoconstricted women (from 19/19 (constricted/dilated) to 2/19 (constricted/dilated)), whereas scores in group 2 remained unchanged. There were no significant changes in blood pressure in either group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Local warming is an effective emergency care measure for acute pelvic pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
